// coverage.js — "a use for every resource" + "every business needs battle loot".
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
// The owner's goal says every one of the game's resources must have a use and every business must
// need things that drop in battle. Measured against the shipped game that is far from true: of the
// 420 catalogue ids, 170 have no consumer of any kind and another ~190 are consumed only by the
// simulated city economy, which keeps its OWN inventory (a unit in a player stash cannot be spent
// there). Just Business ops consume six ids in total. So this file is two things kept strictly apart:
//   LIVE     rows generated from real consumer edges (file:line cite). True in the shipped game.
//   PROPOSED rows hand-authored here. What the owner's map wants. NOT enforced by anything today.
// Every row carries `live`, and the UI must show the difference. Claiming a proposed need is real
// would be a lie to a player deciding whether to found a business.
//
// WHY 'sim' IS ITS OWN ROLE
// A citysim recipe edge is a real line of code, so it is live:true, but it never drains a player's
// stash. If it counted as "a use" the coverage report would look healthy while a player still held
// 190 ids they can do nothing with. needsOf() never returns it and mustWinSimOnly() ignores it.
//
// WHY NO QUANTITIES
// An amount here would be an economy number written down outside OPS_ECON / tuning.js. proposal.js
// turns a proposed need into an {inputs} overlay using SC tiers and opEcon(); this file only says WHAT.
//
// WHY THE NEEDS AGREE WITH THE MAP LANES
// recipes.js says which product rides each lane of the owner's PDF map (Mining -> Construction carries
// metal and stone). The click modal prints that cargo next to needsOf(receiver). For two rounds this
// file listed needs chosen only for theme, and 36 of the 79 shipped cargo ids were not a need of the
// business receiving them: Construction did not need metal. So every lane cargo id is a need row of
// its receiver here, and sc/work/cov/check.mjs fails when that stops being true. coverage.js still
// imports nothing; the agreement is pinned by the test, not by a runtime dependency.
//
// WHY 'trade' IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN
// A player reads "has a use" as "something uses it up". A resale desk is a real business activity, but
// an id whose ONLY owner buys and resells it is still a dead end. Every must-win id therefore has at
// least one input / craft / build / upkeep row; 'trade' rows are extra, never the whole story.
//
// WHAT THE UI MUST NOT OVERPROMISE (owner decision C1)
// About two thirds of the must-win ids are covered by PROPOSED rows only, and most of those are
// battle-loot ids that live in SALVAGE_RES, which no op can spend today. liveSummary() gives a view the
// honest count ("3 of 21 needs are real today") so it never has to guess.
//
// Pure: no DOM, no window, no imports. Node can import() it.

// Phantoms are ids that code REFERS to but no catalogue defines (gunOil is yielded by the oil op and
// exists nowhere else). They are listed so graph.js can flag them; they are never a need.
export const PHANTOMS = ['gunOil', 'sulfur', 'gold', 'organs'];

export const ROLES = ['input', 'craft', 'build', 'upkeep', 'sim', 'trade'];

